## 4. Decompose CF1 into observed CF1, unobserved CF1, and Aggregated CF1

> Fourthly, you can decompose CF1 to observed CF1 and unobserved CF1, and Aggregated CF1.

Before anything else on this item: confirm which existing metric "CF1" names — the
codebase has two candidates and they are not the same thing:
- `PCF` in `run_pcf_eval.py` (2·CC·FC/(CC+FC), an F1-style harmonic mean of
  counterfactual-consistency and factual-consistency; already has a raw/corrected split
  in flight, see experiments/hdae/AGENDA.md §8), or
- `counterfactual_consistency`/`factual_flip_success`/`factual_flip_fail` in the older
  `run_cf_consistency.py` -> `cf_aggregate.py` path (documented as stale/broken in
  experiments/hdae/AGENDA.md §6 — `attribute_classifier` import doesn't exist).

My guess is CF1 = PCF ("Counterfactual-F1"), but I'm not building on that guess without
you confirming it — one wrong assumption here means building the wrong thing.

Plan (assuming CF1 = PCF, pending your confirmation above):
- `run_pcf_eval.py`'s `FC_corr` already subtracts a *correlational* baseline
  (`compute_baselines_and_weights`, naive co-occurrence) so attributes merely correlated
  with the intervened one aren't penalized. This item replaces that correlational
  baseline with item 2's *causal* one: partition the 36 unmodeled attributes into
  "observed" (causal descendants of the intervened attribute per the SCM graph — expected
  to move, scored on whether they moved *as predicted*) and "unobserved" (non-descendants
  — expected to stay put, scored on whether they didn't move).
- `observed_CF1` = F1-style combination of CC with a descendant-specific FC (moved in the
  SCM-predicted direction, not just "moved").
- `unobserved_CF1` = F1-style combination of CC with FC over non-descendants only (like
  today's `FC_corr`, but graph-defined instead of correlation-defined).
- `aggregated_CF1` = the same macro/micro/weighted pattern already implemented for
  `PCF_raw`/`PCF_corr`, extended across the observed/unobserved split.
- Lands entirely inside `run_pcf_eval.py` (or its post-item-1 adapter equivalent) — no new
  files, mostly extends the existing `fc_for()`/aggregation logic.

Depends on: item 2 — hard dependency, needs a causal graph to define "observed" vs
"unobserved" descendants. Must come after.


